Description: directory and single sign-on for a cluster of trusted machines
 Lugh holds the identities of people and services, signs them in with a
 password and a second factor (TOTP, or a passkey or security key), and
 tells every other system who someone is and what they may do: OAuth 2 and
 OpenID Connect for applications, a management API and a dashboard for
 administrators, and LDAP for software that knows nothing else.
 .
 The service is installed disabled: put the TLS pair in /etc/lugh/tls, run
 lugh-node init once to make the keyring and seal the master key, then
 enable lugh.service. The dashboard is served from /usr/share/lugh/dashboard.
